WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.141 --> 00:00:02.515
(static buzzing)
(guitar music)

00:00:02.515 --> 00:00:05.165
- [Narrator] It was spring 1967.

00:00:05.165 --> 00:00:06.776
LBJ was in the White House,

00:00:06.776 --> 00:00:09.144
the Vietnam War was in full swing,

00:00:09.144 --> 00:00:12.075
and the biggest musical act in America was

00:00:12.075 --> 00:00:13.445
The Monkees.

00:00:13.445 --> 00:00:15.784
♫ Hey hey, we're The Monkees

00:00:15.784 --> 00:00:18.355
♫ And people say we monkey around

00:00:18.355 --> 00:00:19.515
- [Narrator] And that same spring,

00:00:19.515 --> 00:00:22.184
a chance encounter in New
York City's Greenwich Village

00:00:22.184 --> 00:00:23.773
would kick-off one of the strangest

00:00:23.773 --> 00:00:26.853
and most ill-fated intersections
of pop culture in history.

00:00:26.853 --> 00:00:29.853
(rock guitar music)

00:00:34.315 --> 00:00:36.336
Here's Monkees drummer Micky Dolenz.

00:00:36.336 --> 00:00:38.344
- Somebody said, you gotta come down

00:00:38.344 --> 00:00:40.885
and see this incredible guitar player

00:00:40.885 --> 00:00:42.534
that plays guitar with his teeth.

00:00:42.534 --> 00:00:45.565
There was no name attached, nothing.

00:00:45.565 --> 00:00:47.895
- [Narrator] That no-name
guitar player was none other

00:00:47.895 --> 00:00:52.062
than rock and roll legend
in the making Jimi Hendrix.

00:00:53.317 --> 00:00:55.642
- Flash forward--
- to June of that year.

00:00:55.642 --> 00:00:58.225
It's the Monterey Pop Festival.

00:00:59.616 --> 00:01:01.264
- [Mickey] And I'm
sitting in the audience.

00:01:01.264 --> 00:01:05.024
All of a sudden, out walks these three

00:01:05.024 --> 00:01:07.691
incredibly decorated characters.

00:01:09.024 --> 00:01:11.064
And I just remember going,

00:01:11.064 --> 00:01:15.064
that's the guy that plays
guitar with his teeth!

00:01:17.864 --> 00:01:22.339
And of course by then they were
the Jimi Hendrix Experience.

00:01:22.339 --> 00:01:24.313
- [Narrator] Micky was impressed.

00:01:24.313 --> 00:01:25.928
- Everybody was blown away.

00:01:25.928 --> 00:01:29.962
And I told our producers,
you gotta check this guy out,

00:01:29.962 --> 00:01:33.504
'cause we were looking for an opening act.

00:01:33.504 --> 00:01:35.704
- [Narrator] And despite some
fairly obvious differences

00:01:35.704 --> 00:01:38.013
in musical style, a deal was struck.

00:01:38.013 --> 00:01:40.682
- [Micky] Sure enough, the
opening act for The Monkees

00:01:40.682 --> 00:01:43.349
was the Jimi Hendrix Experience.

00:01:44.264 --> 00:01:46.784
- [Newscaster] A new group,
Jimi Hendrix Experience,

00:01:46.784 --> 00:01:48.634
traveling with The
Monkees and they've joined

00:01:48.634 --> 00:01:50.194
The Monkees' concert tour.

00:01:50.194 --> 00:01:52.373
- [Narrator] So the two acts hit the road.

00:01:52.373 --> 00:01:53.673
But, as it turned out,

00:01:53.673 --> 00:01:55.333
fans who had come to see The Monkees

00:01:55.333 --> 00:01:57.803
weren't quite sure what
to make of Jimi Hendrix.

00:01:57.803 --> 00:01:59.685
- So he would go on stage to open the act.

00:01:59.685 --> 00:02:01.285
And he'd be goin'

00:02:01.285 --> 00:02:04.495
♫ Foxy Lady (imitates guitar playing)

00:02:04.495 --> 00:02:05.578
We want Davy!

00:02:06.736 --> 00:02:08.816
We want The Monkees!

00:02:08.816 --> 00:02:10.976
It was kind of embarrassing, frankly.

00:02:10.976 --> 00:02:12.274
- [Narrator] And things
didn't get much better

00:02:12.274 --> 00:02:13.816
as the tour continued.

00:02:13.816 --> 00:02:16.456
And after only seven
shows, Jimi had had enough.

00:02:16.456 --> 00:02:18.696
- I did hear the rumor
at one of the last shows

00:02:18.696 --> 00:02:20.954
he'd finally flipped off the audience.

00:02:20.954 --> 00:02:23.153
(chuckling) I don't blame him, frankly.

00:02:23.153 --> 00:02:24.266
- [Narrator] And just like that,

00:02:24.266 --> 00:02:26.554
the unlikely marriage of
Hendrix and The Monkees

00:02:26.554 --> 00:02:28.164
had come to an end.

00:02:28.164 --> 00:02:31.184
Jimi of course went on to
be, well, Jimi Hendrix.

00:02:31.184 --> 00:02:34.194
- Possibly the greatest
rock and roll guitarist

00:02:34.194 --> 00:02:36.496
ever, as far as I'm concerned.

00:02:36.496 --> 00:02:39.026
I'd like to think that we might have had

00:02:39.026 --> 00:02:41.426
something to do with it, but I think

00:02:41.426 --> 00:02:42.299
he would have done okay

00:02:42.299 --> 00:02:45.135
with or without opening for The Monkees.

00:02:45.135 --> 00:02:47.552
(rock music)

00:02:51.633 --> 00:02:54.550
(electronic music)

